From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jaime Dias <jaimejosediasnt@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 30 Aug 2026 00:00:00 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] Let a Bellatrix guest fault at PC=0 instead of stopping

PC = 0 ends MainLoop, which is right for the bare-metal test executables
Emu68 also runs and wrong for a machine booting an operating system. A
guest that reaches 0 there has called through a library or device base
that was never filled in, and ending the loop turns that into a stopped
machine with no explanation: AROS never runs its own trap handler, never
prints its crash requester or the stack trace this port keeps frame
pointers for, and the log shows a clean "Back from translated code".

A day was spent reading one of those as a fault of the port. The same
demo, started the same way on a real Kickstart in the Rigel harness,
answers "Software error - task held" -- the operating system catching
what this machine could not.

Every path a fault then takes already exists and is already exercised.
What changes is who explains the crash.

The condition is BELLATRIX_EMU68 alone, not BELLATRIX_EMU68 && CONFIG_RIGEL.
The argument above is about booting an operating system, and a Bellatrix
image built or booted without the chipset boots the same one; tying it to
Rigel would have made a runtime chipset switch silently change something
that has nothing to do with the chipset.
---
 src/ExecutionLoop.c | 28 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 27 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/src/ExecutionLoop.c b/src/ExecutionLoop.c
--- a/src/ExecutionLoop.c
+++ b/src/ExecutionLoop.c
@@ -317,9 +317,36 @@
         /* Load m68k context and last used PC counter into temporary register */ 
         LastPC = getLastPC();
         ctx = getCTX();
 
-#ifndef PISTORM_ANY_MODEL
+/*
+ * PC = 0 is "the program finished" only for a machine that was given a
+ * program to finish.
+ *
+ * Emu68 runs bare-metal test executables and ends the loop when they return
+ * to a null address, which is the right convention for those. Bellatrix boots
+ * an operating system, and a guest that reaches 0 there has done something
+ * else entirely: it has called through a library or device base that was
+ * never filled in -- the unchecked OpenLibrary or OpenDevice, which is one of
+ * the oldest bugs on this platform.
+ *
+ * Ending the loop turns that into a stopped machine with no explanation. AROS
+ * never gets to run its own trap handler, so it never prints the crash
+ * requester and the stack trace this port keeps frame pointers for, and the
+ * log shows a clean "Back from translated code" that reads like a normal
+ * exit. A whole day was spent reading one as a fault of the port when the
+ * same demo, started the same way on a real Kickstart in the Rigel harness,
+ * answers "Software error - task held" -- the operating system catching what
+ * this machine could not.
+ *
+ * So let it fault. A 68000 that jumps to 0 executes what is at 0 and traps
+ * shortly after, and every path that then runs -- illegal instruction, bus
+ * error, the guest's own vector table -- already exists here and is already
+ * exercised. What changes is who explains the crash: the guest, which knows
+ * which task it was and where it came from, instead of a register dump from
+ * outside.
+ */
+#if !defined(PISTORM_ANY_MODEL) && !defined(BELLATRIX_EMU68)
         if (unlikely(PC == 0)) {
             M68K_SaveContext(ctx);
             return;
         }
